From tru64-unix-managers  Wed Mar 28 21:25:46 2001
From: Kevin Dea <kdea () alpine-la ! com>
Date: Wed, 28 Mar 2001 21:25:46 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: write permission with emacs
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=98581477417386


Hello Managers,

First off, I'd like to thank Alan Davis, Calvin Coghlan, Anthony A. D.
Talltree, sysadmin@astro.su.se, and David Henden for their suggestions.

Almost everyone suggested that I check the permissions on the directory,
because emacs drops in a backup *~ file into the directory.  I had
neglected to mention in my original post that the directory was already
set to 775, and owned by the owner of the file in question.  That owner
and the programmer wanting to modify the file was in the same group
(users).  I think that Alan Davis had the correct reason:

[Davis, Alan wrote]
 
> Check to see if the file is being managed by RCS. Emacs is RCS aware and the
> RCS status will over-ride the filesystem permissions.

I asked the programmer if the file was managed by RCS and it was by
managed CVS.  Although I don't understand why I was able to modify the
file as non-root.  My original post follows.

Thanks,

