)}}\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {We are testing with =
an U.S. Robotics 56K Faxmodem connected to COM2 on a small Alpha =
Server.=20
\par=20
\par The following steps were taken on the Alpha Server to configure it =
for Remote Access.
\par=20
\par First,  make sure that the COM port device special files had been =
made correctly in /dev; to check perform the following command:
\par \tab file /dev/tty0*
\par \tab /dev/tty00:     character special (35/0)
\par }\pard \fi720\widctlpar\adjustright {/dev/tty01:     character =
special (35/1) ACE_SLU #0 terminal #1 modem_control on
\par=20
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {If these files do not exist, then =
they need to be created; to create these files cd to /dev and perform =
the following command:
\par \tab ./MAKEDEV ace0\tab=20
\par=20
\par Ensure that the modem is connected to the correct COM port.=20
\par=20
\par Add the following string}{\cgrid0  to the /etc/remote file for =
testing and port speed initialization:}{\f2\cgrid0=20
\par }{\tab }{\cgrid0 tty01:dv=3D/dev/tty01:br#9600:pa=3Dnone:}{
\par=20
\par }{\cgrid0 Make certain that the modem is set to allow DTR and =
Carrier Detect to be controlled by the host and the modem respectively, =
particularly important when setting up a dialin connection.  Also make =
sure that autoanswer is enabled for di
alin connections.   These can be controlled by either dip switch =
settings, software registers or through a combination of both.  Elsa =
modem may differ slightly.}{\f2\cgrid0=20
\par }{
\par=20
\par Currently, the DIP switch settings we are using are UUUUUUUD:
\par=20
\par 1 U DTR Normal / Turns off DTR Override
\par 2 U Verbal results
\par 3 U Suppress result codes
\par 4 U Echo Off-line commands / Displays keyboard commands
\par 5 U Enables Auto Answer on first ring or value in S0
\par 6 U Carrier Detect Normal / Turns off CD Override=20
\par 7 U Loads NVRAM defaults=20
\par 8 D Smart Mode / Enables AT Command set recognition=20
\par=20
\par Test }{\cgrid0 the modem connection.  Use the following command:
\par }\pard \fi720\widctlpar\adjustright {\cgrid0 tip tty01
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\cgrid0 The host should respond =
with the string "CONNECTED". At this point you can then issue AT =
commands to the modem. =20
\par=20
\par If you don't see commands echoed, then either you are not =
connecting to the correct port, or echo has been disabled on the modem. =
While connected via tip, type the command ATE1.  This should enable =
local command echo.
\par=20
\par Now check the modem register values for &C &D and &S.  Some modems =
will show all of the registers with an ATI4 command.  Other modems =
require you to display them individually.
\par=20
\par \page The following output is from the ATI4 command on our USR =
modem:
\par=20
\par U.S. Robotics 56K FAX EXT Settings...
\par=20
\par    B0  E1  F1  M1  Q1  V1  X4  Y0
\par    BAUD=3D9600  PARITY=3DN  WORDLEN=3D8
\par    DIAL=3DTONE    ON HOOK   CID=3D0
\par=20
\par    &A0  &B1  &C1  &D2  &G0  &H1  &I0  &K1
\par    &M4  &N0  &P1  &R2  &S1  &T5  &U0  &Y1
\par=20
\par    S00=3D001  S01=3D000  S02=3D043  S03=3D013  S04=3D010  =
S05=3D008  S06=3D004
\par    S07=3D060  S08=3D002  S09=3D006  S10=3D014  S11=3D070  =
S12=3D050  S13=3D000
\par    S15=3D000  S16=3D000  S18=3D000  S19=3D000  S21=3D010  =
S22=3D017  S23=3D019
\par    S25=3D005  S27=3D000  S28=3D008  S29=3D020  S30=3D000  =
S31=3D128  S32=3D002
\par    S33=3D000  S34=3D000  S35=3D000  S36=3D014  S38=3D000  =
S39=3D000  S40=3D001
\par    S41=3D000  S42=3D000
\par=20
\par    LAST DIALED #:
\par=20
\par AT$ should provide help and a command quick reference for all =
values.
\par=20
\par The following commands were used to change values on our test =
modem:
\par }\pard \li720\widctlpar\adjustright {\cgrid0 AT&D2\tab DTE =
Controls DTR
\par AT&C1\tab Modem Controls CD
\par AT&S1\tab Modem Controls DSR
\par AT&H1\tab CTS
\par ATF1\tab No Online Echo
\par AT&A0\tab Disable /ARQ Result Codes Bingo!!! This one did the =
trick.}{\f2\cgrid0=20
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\f2\cgrid0=20
\par }{\cgrid0 In most cases &C and &D will be correct by default, but =
they should be set to allow "Normal" CD and DTR operation respectively.
\par=20
\par The register &S controls DSR and may need to be changed to allow =
the modem to control DSR. If so, then change the value.
\par=20
\par When completed, save the registers to allow them to survive a =
power cycle of the modem.  This was done with   or AT&W0, but some =
modems may require a different command.
\par=20
\par After saving the modem configuration, exit tip, by typing tilde., =
\ldblquote ~.\rdblquote }{\f2\cgrid0=20
\par=20
\par }{\cgrid0 For a dialin connection, it is necessary to have a getty =
process running against the tty device.  This process is started by the =
init process and so the following line must be added to the =
/etc/inittab file:
\par \tab /etc/inittab:\tab tty01:23:respawn:/usr/sbin/getty /dev/tty01 =
M9600 vt100
\par=20
\par Once the changes to /etc/inittab have been made, issue the =
command:
\par }\pard \fi720\widctlpar\adjustright {\cgrid0 init q
\par }\pard \widctlpar\adjustright {\cgrid0 This command will cause the =
init process to reread the inittab file. This process should then spawn =
the getty which will start the login process when an interrupt is seen =
from the modem attached to the port.
\par=20
\par }\pard\plain =
\s2\sb240\sa60\keepn\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\i\f1\cgrid =
{\cgrid0 Note
\par }\pard\plain \widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\cgrid {\cgrid0 We will =
probably need to configure PPP or SLIP at some point, in the very near =
future and I will send some additional information regarding this =
configuration after I research it a bit more.
\par }{
