The problem is solved by using the second method as described below
by James Sainsbury. It worked fine.

Thanks to all of you those who have replied, especially Nik Tarasyuk
and James Sainsbury. -   Tapan

---------- Forwarded message ----------
Date: Tue, 27 Mar 2001 14:37:44 +1000 (EST)
From: James Sainsbury <sainsb_j@chem.usyd.edu.au>
To: "Tapan K. Nayak" <nayak@veccal.ernet.in>
Subject: Re: Booting of AlphaStation 500/333

Try booting your generic kernel first.

>>> boot dka0 -fl s -fi genvmunix 
(assuming dka0 is your boot disk otherwise  dka100 dka200 etc )
and copy the kernel from /sys/KERNEL/vmunix (as below.)

else from your install CD get to your shell
cd  /dev
./MAKEDEV rz0
# if rz0 is your boot disk. Use rz1 etc if different 
fsck -p /dev/rz0a
mount /dev/rz0a /mnt
cd /mnt/sys/KERNEL
cp vmunix /
cd /
umount /mnt 
# where KERNEL is the name of your kernel usually the hostname.
reboot

RGDS

